  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA storm system is expected to track across the Midwest and Ohio\nValley on Wednesday into Wednesday night. By Thursday, the low \npressure area looks to track just south of the area across the \nnorthern mid Atlantic before passing close to the coast of Long \nIsland and southern New England for Thursday night. As a large \nupper level trough approaches from the west, this low pressure \narea will slowly sit and spin close to the coast of eastern New \nEngland on Friday before exiting into the western Atlantic Ocean\nby Friday night. There still remains some uncertainty regarding\njust how quick the storm exits for Thursday night into Friday, \nas it will depend on the interaction with the approaching upper \nlevel low. \n\nA period of warm advection will lead to the initial light\nprecipitation for Wednesday night into early Thursday. This\nlooks to be rain to start across the entire region. However, the\ncombination of wet-bulbing and dynamic cooling with allow for\nprecipitation to change over to snow across the high terrain of\nthe Adirondacks during the day on Thursday. Cooling temps aloft\nthanks to the approaching upper level trough will allow for more\nof the higher terrain of the region to see a change over to \nsnow for Thursday night into Friday. Some models suggests that \nsome banding precipitation within the deformation could even \nallow for precip in valley areas to switch over to snow as well \nby Friday morning, although this will depend on the exact track \nand strength of the storm system and if enough cold air can \narrive in time. Heavier bands of precip could help allow for \ndynamic cooling to flip p-type over to snow as well. Steady\nlight precip looks to occur during the day on Friday as an\ninverted trough sets up. Eventually, precip will tend to taper \nto mainly snow showers and flurries by late Friday into Friday \nnight, as the surface low departs and the upper level trough \nmoves overhead. \n\nModel guidance has been showing varying solutions lately, but \nmuch of the guidance has been hinting at this potential for more\nwintry precip over the past few runs. Some runs of the RRFS \nhave shown a light to moderate accumulation in valley areas, \nwith moderate to heavy accumulation across the high terrain.\nWith these models being rather new and this being a unique early\nseason event, there is somewhat limited confidence in how this\nnewer guidance will handle this system. In addition, there has \nbeen vastly different solutions with each run, which also limits\nconfidence.\n\nThe WPC WSO from earlier this evening been fairly low (10-30%),\nalthough it may start to jump up with the newer guidance. We\nhave been leaning towards the probabilistic WSSI from WPC which\nsuggests a 30-50% chance of minor impacts across the high \nterrain at this time. As newer guidance arrives, there numbers \nare likely to change, so this is a rather fluid situation. The \nNBM probabilities are just starting to rise, so will continue \nto closely monitor those numbers as well. For now, the\nAdirondacks look to be the most-favored and at least an advisory\nwill probably be needed there and perhaps across southern VT as\nwell. Elsewhere, it's a big question mark on how much, if any, \nsnow will actually accumulate. This will likely come into better\nfocus over the next day or so.",
